  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 238 AM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nA brief reprieve from scattered thunderstorms is anticipated Friday \nas the upper-level start to transitioning to another active pattern. \nThe upper-level ridge remains in place Friday, though starts to move \noff to the east and tilt towards the northeast Friday night into \nearly Saturday morning. The 500mb high over the Four Corners \ncontinues to spin through the afternoon hours Friday, but gets \npushed off to the south and east Friday night into early Saturday \nmorning. 700mb flow turns more westerly Friday, as the ridge starts \nto weaken and move out of the region. A brief period of increased \nheight gradients at 700mb will promote slightly stronger 700mb \nwinds, with GFS Omega field suggesting modest downward motion along \nand east of the Laramie Range Friday morning through mid-day. Much \nas is expected for Wyoming during the year, the slightly stronger \n700mb flow and increased downward omega values will lead to stronger \nsurface winds, especially across Carbon County where strong \nsouthwesterly flow is anticipated to develop. ALl areas have less \nthan a 30% chance at high winds during this period, but gusty winds \nlook quite likely. 700mb temperatures also increase for Friday as \nthe apex of the trough moves directly overhead then off to the east. \n700mb temperatures will warm into the 15 to 19C range Friday \nafternoon. Surface temperatures look to max out in the upper-80s to \nupper-90s in all locations east of the Laramie Range. These very \nwarm temperatures combined with gusty downsloping winds along and \neast of the Laramie Range, as well as minimal precipitation chances, \nwill lead to elevated fire weather conditions as afternoon minimum \nhumidity values drop into the 15 to 20 percent range across much of \nthe area. Red Flag conditions are not anticipated at this time, but \nvery dry and windy conditions are expected for the afternoon hours.\n\nSaturday onwards will be dominated by the upper-level trough moving \ninto the Pacific Northwest Friday through the weekend. Active \nweather looks to continue Saturday onwards with the much more \nprogressive upper-level flow. Daily, scattered to numerous showers \nand storms are expected Saturday and Sunday afternoons as the upper-\nlevel trough pushes into the region, increasing synoptic forcing. \nSunday looks particularly interesting as the upper-level trough \nstarts to become negatively tilted over central Montana and a strong \njet develops from eastern Montana southeastward through western \nNebraska. Much of the region will be in the right entrance region of \nthe strong, upper-level jet, favoring much stronger forcing across \nthe region Sunday afternoon. While this setup is still several days \nout, long range models suggest decent 700mb WAA across the region \nSunday afternoon. When combined with the strong, upper-level jet, \nstorms could become quite strong Sunday afternoon. Details for this \nsetup will become clearer over the coming days. Active weather will \ncontinue through the end of the long term period with daily shower \nand thunderstorms chances across the region. Surface temperatures \nwill be quite warm Saturday before cooling off Sunday. More \nseasonable temperatures expected Monday through the end of the long \nterm forecast period.",
